"Casting inorganic structures with DNA molds - PubMed (https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25301973/)\nciteturn75search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 12.0 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; # Casting inorganic structures with DNA molds\n\nWei Sun^{  1 }, Etienne Boulais^{  2 }, Yera Hakobyan^{  2 }, Wei Li Wang^{  1 }, Amy Guan^{  3 }, Mark Bathe^{  4 }, Peng Yin^{  5 } ... Computationally designed DNA strands self-assemble into a stiff \"nanomold\" that contains a user-specified three-dimensional cavity and encloses a nucleating gold \"seed.\" Under mild conditions, this seed grows into a larger cast structure that fills and thus replicates the cavity.\n\n. 2014 Nov 7;346(6210):1258361.\n\ndoi: 10.1126/science.1258361. Epub 2014 Oct 9.\n\n# Casting inorganic structures with DNA molds\n\nWei Sun^{  1 }, Etienne Boulais^{  2 }, Yera Hakobyan^{  2 }, Wei Li Wang^{  1 }, Amy Guan^{  3 }, Mark Bathe^{  4 }, Peng Yin^{  5 }\n\n\n### Affiliations\n\n  * ^{1} Wyss Institute for Biologically Inspired Engineering, Harvard University, Boston, MA 02115, USA. Department of Systems Biology, Harvard Medical School, Boston, MA 02115, USA.\n  * ^{2} Department of Biological Engineering, Massachusetts Institute of Technology, Cambridge, MA 02139, USA.\n  * ^{3} Wyss Institute for Biologically Inspired Engineering, Harvard University, Boston, MA 02115, USA.\n  * ^{4} Department of Biological Engineering, Massachusetts Institute of Technology, Cambridge, MA 02139, USA. py@hms.harvard.edu mark.bathe@mit.edu.\n  * ^{5} Wyss Institute for Biologically Inspired Engineering, Harvard University, Boston, MA 02115, USA. Department of Systems Biology, Harvard Medical School, Boston, MA 02115, USA. py@hms.harvard.edu mark.bathe@mit.edu.\n\n  * PMID: 25301973\n  * PMCID: PMC4260265\n  * DOI: 10.1126/science.1258361\n\nItem in Clipboard\n\n# Casting inorganic structures with DNA molds\n\nWei Sun et al. Science. 2014.\n\n\n. 2014 Nov 7;346(6210):1258361.\n\ndoi: 10.1126/science.1258361. Epub 2014 Oct 9.\n\n### Authors\n\nWei Sun^{  1 }, Etienne Boulais^{  2 }, Yera Hakobyan^{  2 }, Wei Li Wang^{  1 }, Amy Guan^{  3 }, Mark Bathe^{  4 }, Peng Yin^{  5 }\n\n### Affiliations\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe report a general strategy for designing and synthesizing inorganic nanostructures with arbitrarily prescribed three-dimensional shapes. Computationally designed DNA strands self-assemble into a stiff \"nanomold\" that contains a user-specified three-dimensional cavity and encloses a nucleating gold \"seed.\" Under mild conditions, this seed grows into a larger cast structure that fills and thus replicates the cavity. We synthesized a variety of nanoparticles with 3-nanometer resolution: three distinct silver cuboids with three independently tunable dimensions, silver and gold nanoparticles with diverse cross sections, and composite structures with homo- and heterogeneous components. The designer equilateral silver triangular and spherical nanoparticles exhibited plasmonic properties consistent with electromagnetism-based simulations. Our framework is generalizable to more complex geometries and diverse inorganic materials, offering a range of applications in biosensing, photonics, and nanoelectronics.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCasting inorganic structures with DNA molds - PMC (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4260265/)\nciteturn75search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 12.0 years ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; # Casting inorganic structures with DNA molds ... The DNA mold served as an addressable coating for the cast NP and thus enabled the construction of higher order composite structures, including a Y-shaped Ag NP composite and a quantum-dot (QD)-Ag-QD sandwiched structure.\n\n. Author manuscript; available in PMC: 2015 May 7.\n\nPublished in final edited form as: Science. 2014 Oct 9;346(6210):1258361. doi: 10.1126/science.1258361\n\n# Casting inorganic structures with DNA molds\n\nIssue date 2014 Nov 7.\n\nPMCID: 