rands self-assemble into a stiff “nanomold” that contains a user-specified three-dimensional cavity and encloses a nucleating gold “seed.” Under mild conditions, this seed grows into a larger cast structure that fills and thus replicates the cavity. We synthesized a variety of nanoparticles with 3-nanometer resolution: three distinct silver cuboids with three independently tunable dimensions, silver and gold nanoparticles with diverse cross sections, and composite structures with homo- and heterogeneous components. The designer equilateral silver triangular and spherical nanoparticles exhibited plasmonic properties consistent with electromagnetism-based simulations. Our framework is generalizable to more complex geometries and diverse inorganic materials, offering a range of applications in biosensing, photonics, and nanoelectronics.\n\nMIT Department\n\nMassachusetts Institute of Technology. Department of Biological Engineering\n\nTerms of Use\n\nCreative Commons Attribution-Noncommercial-Share Alike\n\nhttp://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/\n\nPersistent DSpace Link\n\nhttp://hdl.handle.net/1721.1/99234\n\nDOI of Published Version\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1126/science.1258361--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSun_pages 1..8 (https://yin.hms.harvard.edu/publications/2014.casting.pdf)\nciteturn75search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5 months ago; Casting inorganic structures with\nDNA molds\nWei Sun,1,2 Etienne Boulais,3 Yera Hakobyan,3 Wei Li Wang,1,2 Amy Guan,1 ... state solution structure of the DNA mold adopted\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nwww.sciencemag.org/cgi/content/full/science.1258361/DC1 (https://yin.hms.harvard.edu/publications/2014.casting.sup1.pdf)\nciteturn75search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 12.1 years ago; Casting inorganic structures with DNA molds \nWei Sun, Etienne Boulais, Yera Hakobyan, Wei Li Wang, Amy Guan, Mark Bathe,* Peng Yin*\n-